# Living Survey: Artificial_General_Intelligence                                                                
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
## Introduction                                                                                                 
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
This document collects the scientific literature regarding **Artificial_General_Intelligence**.                 
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
## Theoretical Foundations & Cognitive Architectures                                                            
                                                                                                                
Recent scholarship has begun to rigorously redefine the architectural boundaries of AGI. Bradley (2026) proposes
a morphogenetic topological framework, arguing that autonomy and consciousness emerge from the structural       
preservation of a "Form F" against dissolution, mathematically framing AGI as an inevitable geometric property  
rather than a computational byproduct [^10.5281_zenodo.19034234]. Complementing this, Yampolskiy (2024)         
challenges the conflation of AGI with Human-Level AI (HLAI), proving that human cognition lacks the true        
generality required for AGI, thereby necessitating distinct architectural paradigms [^10.1201_9781003440260-12].
Neubauer (2021) further explores this distinction, contrasting human versus artificial intelligence concepts    
while examining how cognitive enhancements and individual psychological differences could shape the development 
of super-intelligent systems [^10.1016_j.intell.2021.101563].                                                   
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
## Safety, Alignment & Control                                                                                  
                                                                                                                
As AGI capabilities expand, ensuring provable safety has emerged as a critical research frontier. Tegmark and   
Omohundro (2023) argue that formal verification and mechanistic interpretability, powered by advanced AI itself,
represent the only viable path to guaranteeing that powerful AGI systems satisfy human-specified requirements   
without catastrophic failure [^10.48550_arxiv.2309.01933]. This technical safety imperative is closely tied to  
the broader question of machine consciousness.                                                                  
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
## Ethics, Policy & Societal Impact                                                                             
                                                                                                                
The trajectory of AGI development is heavily influenced by regulatory frameworks and ethical critiques. Stahl   
and Eke (2026) critique the European Union's recent calls for AGI in healthcare, warning that the ambiguous and 
contested nature of the term AGI conflicts with the EU's precautionary principle, potentially masking           
unquantifiable risks behind funding initiatives [^10.1016_j.jrt.2026.100183]. Gebru and Torres (2024) draw a    
direct lineage between modern AGI safety rhetoric and historical Anglo-American eugenics, arguing that the      
pursuit of an undefined "beneficial AGI" centralizes power and perpetuates systemic discrimination under the    
guise of safety [^10.5210_fm.v29i4.13636]. On a macro-societal level, Hutter and Hutter (2021) outline two      
divergent futures: one where uncontrolled AI substitution leads to severe inequality and social fragmentation,  
and another where automation liberates humanity, fostering ethical self-determination and humane governance     
[^10.3390_asi4020037].                                                                                          
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
## Robotics, Control Systems & Neural Architectures                                                             
                                                                                                                
Translating AGI theory into physical autonomy requires robust control frameworks. Dargazany (2021) introduces   
Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) as a platform-independent, application-agnostic framework for intelligent     
robot control, decoupling high-level intelligence generation from low-level kinematic execution to enable       
scalable AGI deployment [^10.48550_arxiv.2105.13806]. Parallel to this, Zhuravlev and Smolin (2023) examine the 
neural network revolution, arguing that moving beyond the imitation of human problem-solving toward             
environmental interaction and knowledge accumulation is essential for bridging the gap between narrow AI and    
genuine general intelligence [^10.20948_future-2023-16].                                                        
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
## Geopolitical & Strategic Development                                                                         
                                                                                                                
The race for AGI is increasingly framed as a matter of national strategy. Hannas et al. (2022) document China's 
comprehensive national strategy to achieve leadership in "general AI" by 2030, tracking thirty institutions     
actively pursuing machine learning, brain-inspired architectures, and brain-computer interfaces to enable       
autonomous operation in novel environments [^10.51593_20210064].                                                
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                
## Benchmarking & Evaluation                                                                                    
                                                                                                                
Evaluating AGI capabilities requires moving beyond static input-output pairs toward dynamic, multi-dimensional  
benchmarks. Beger et al. (2026) introduce PotARCin, a benchmark that assesses abstract rule understanding across
five dimensions (Definition, Classification, Constrained Generation, Editing, and Inversion), revealing a       
massive performance gap between standard ARC evaluation and holistic rule comprehension [^2609.27288].          
Similarly, Potapov (2026) presents the AGI Maze Prediction Datasets, a controlled testbed for studying world    
dynamics in Transformers, demonstrating that structured, task-aligned working memory significantly outperforms  
unstructured latent capacity in learning transferable action-conditioned dynamics [^2609.02339].                
                                                                                                                
## Multi-Agent & Socio-Affective Systems                                                                        
                                                                                                                
The shift toward collaborative and socially-aware AGI architectures is gaining traction. Berga (2026) outlines  
the AGIMUD software architecture, which integrates socially-aware reasoning, emotion modeling, and distributed  
processing to enable real-time human-agent interactions in simulated dynamic worlds [^2609.26927]. Park et al.  
(2026) demonstrate that test-time communication among multi-agent teams substantially outperforms independent   
parallel attempts on complex reasoning tasks like ARC-AGI-3, showing that sharing breakthroughs allows teams to 
solve tasks that no single agent could achieve alone [^2609.21032].                                             
                                                                                                                
## In-Context Learning & World Modeling                                                                         
                                                                                                                
Leveraging in-context observations for world modeling has proven highly effective for AGI reasoning. Matsutani  
et al. (2026) show that few-shot demonstrations from diverse worlds relocate and enhance predictive world       
representations in LLMs, significantly boosting performance on ARC-AGI-1&2 and web agent tasks [^2609.24352].   
Engdahl et al. (2026) introduce BDH-CQ, a reasoning model combining in-context learning with recurrent latent   
reasoning, which continuously updates its memory at inference time to achieve a new cost-accuracy Pareto        
frontier on ARC-AGI-1 [^2608.09888].                                                                            
                                                                                                                
## Self-Improving & Recursive Systems                                                                           
                                                                                                                
Recursive self-improvement and persistent execution harnesses are pushing the boundaries of autonomous AGI      
agents. Kim et al. (2026) present Meta^n, a system that recursively applies a fixed meta-operation to its own   
outputs, achieving emergent depth and outperforming prior self-improving agents on ARC-AGI-2 without            
destabilizing the editing machinery [^2608.24735]. Complementing this, Karten et al. (2026) introduce Prime     
Agent, an open-source harness for long-horizon recursive language model workflows that preserves memories and   
skills across trajectories, raising ARC-AGI-3 performance from 30% to 95.5% through continuous refinement and   
subagent coordination [^2608.23552].                                                                            
                                                                                                                
## Mechanistic Interpretability                                                                                 
                                                                                                                
Understanding the internal mechanics of reasoning models is critical for reliable AGI. Rodrigues and Kang (2026)
conduct a mechanistic study of Hierarchical Reasoning Models (HRM) on ARC-AGI-2, revealing that recurrent states
implement constraint-aware iterative refinement rather than relying on compact, causally important feature sets,
highlighting the need for interpretability techniques tailored to latent-space recursive reasoning              
[^2608.28824].                                                                                                  
                                                                                                                
## Multimodal & Visual AGI                                                                                      
                                                                                                                
Vision is increasingly recognized as a potential primary pathway to AGI. Kataoka et al. (2026) publish a white  
paper on Visual General Intelligence (VGI), arguing that intelligence emerging from visual experience and       
learning may provide a more robust foundation than language alone, and outlining core principles, benchmarks,   
and learning paradigms for vision-centered AGI development [^2608.25924].                                       
                                                                                                                
## World Modeling & Simulation                                                                                  
                                                                                                                
Constructing executable world models from interaction alone represents a major leap in autonomous reasoning.    
Skoutnev et al. (2026) introduce Twin, a system where a coding agent writes an executable world model to        
complete continual learning tasks like ARC-AGI-3 games, achieving 97.8% level clearance by inferring game rules 
and goals through simulation and replay validation [^2608.14490].                                               
                                                                                                                
## Hybrid Reasoning Architectures                                                                               
                                                                                                                
Combining symbolic search with neural generation offers promising results for complex reasoning. Dyachenko      
(2026) demonstrates that seeding genetic algorithms with programs generated by a quantized LLM dramatically     
improves success rates on ARC-AGI-2, solving 10% of tasks compared to 3.3% with LLMs alone, proving that genetic
search can effectively refine neural outputs [^2609.27242].                                                     
                                                                                                                

